\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Two different steps}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{The time constant of sodium activation is much smaller
than the time constants of potassium activation and of
inactivation.}

\p{\citet{§3.6, pp. 66–67}{ermentrout2010mathematical} replaces
#{m} by #{m_{\infty}(V)}. That replacement is the
zeroth-order slow manifold. These notes do not yet state
the persistence of a nearby invariant manifold for a small
time-scale ratio, away from folds.}

\p{The other step is an empirical fit.
\citet{§5.2.1, pp. 148–149}{izhikevich2007dynamical} records
#{n+h\approx 0.84} and the line #{h=0.89-1.1n}.}

\p{\citet{p. 67}{ermentrout2010mathematical} writes the same
observation as #{n=b-rh}, after Krinskii and Kokoz, and
records that the slope and the intercept move with the
current. Replacing a fast variable by its steady value does
not justify a line between #{n} and #{h}.}

\p{\citet{pp. 67–68}{ermentrout2010mathematical} compares
the variables as voltages, after Kepler, Abbott and Marder.
Sodium activation stays with #{V}, and potassium activation
stays with inactivation, so the reduced pair is
#{(V,V_{h})}. The lower Hopf point of that pair is
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}\approx 6.8}, and the second Hopf point is at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=267}. The full model has its upper Hopf
point near #{I_{\mathrm{app}}\approx 154}, as
\ref{fnip-0103} records. The #{V_{h}}-nullcline is the
diagonal #{V=V_{h}}, and the #{V}-nullcline is cubic.}

\p{\citet{(3.5)–(3.6), p. 69}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
is the cubic caricature of that nullcline,
##{\dot V=V(V-a)(1-V)-w+I,}
##{\dot w=\varepsilon(V-\gamma w),}
with #{0<a<1}, #{\varepsilon>0}, and #{\gamma\ge 0}.}
